= Taxonomy — Technical =

== Graph structure ==
A taxonomy can be modeled as directed graph:

<pre>
T = (C, B)
</pre>

where C is a set of concepts/categories and B a broader-than relation.

A tree requires each non-root concept to have one parent.

A polyhierarchy relaxes this to allow several parents.

== Transitivity ==
A broader relation may support a transitive closure:

<pre>
A narrowerThan B
B narrowerThan C
→ A narrowerTransitive C
</pre>

But only if the hierarchy uses a semantically coherent broader relation.

Mixed part-of/topic/subclass edges can invalidate ordinary inheritance.

== SKOS ==
SKOS distinguishes direct semantic relations and transitive superproperties.

Concept organization remains intentionally weaker than formal OWL class semantics.

This makes SKOS suitable for thesauri/taxonomies without requiring strong ontological commitment.

== Mapping ==
Taxonomy alignment can use:
* exact match;
* close match;
* broad match;
* narrow match;
* related match.

Mappings should preserve uncertainty instead of promoting all lexical similarity to equivalence.

== Evolution ==
Taxonomy versioning needs to handle:
* renames;
* merges;
* splits;
* moves;
* new broader/narrower relations;
* retired concepts.

Stable identifiers help preserve reference through label changes.

